.Dd September 19, 2026
.Dt BUSYBOX-SYSKLOGD 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm busybox-sysklogd ,
.Nm klogd ,
.Nm logger ,
.Nm logread ,
.Nm syslogd
.Nd BusyBox log tools
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm klogd
.Op Fl c Ar N
.Op Fl n
.Nm logger
.Op Fl s
.Op Fl t Ar TAG
.Op Fl p Ar PRIO
.Op Ar MESSAGE
.Nm logread
.Op Fl fF
.Nm syslogd
.Op Ar OPTIONS
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These are the sysklogd applets of BusyBox 1.36.1 as built for Rach2.
The kernel's own log is
.Pa /dev/bootlog ;
see
.Xr bootlog 4 .
.Bl -tag -width syslogd
.It Nm klogd
Log kernel messages to syslog.
It uses
.Fn klogctl ,
which Rach2 does not implement: its read fails and it exits.
.It Nm logger
Write
.Ar MESSAGE ,
or standard input, to syslog.
On Rach2 the message is not delivered, since nothing listens at
.Pa /dev/log ;
with
.Fl s
it still goes to standard error.
.It Nm logread
Display messages in the circular buffer of
.Nm syslogd .
.It Nm syslogd
Provide system logging.
On Rach2 it cannot start: it binds a Unix domain socket at
.Pa /dev/log ,
and
.Pa /dev
is a synthetic file system that takes no new node, so the bind fails with
.Er EROFS ;
and
.Nm logread
needs the System V IPC the kernel does not have.
.El
.Sh OPTIONS
.Ss klogd
.Bl -tag -width "-c N"
.It Fl c Ar N
Print messages more urgent than priority
.Ar N
to the console; without effect on Rach2, where the daemon does not run.
The priority is from 1 to 8.
.It Fl n
Run in the foreground.
.El
.Ss logger
.Bl -tag -width "-p PRIO"
.It Fl s
Log to standard error as well as the system log.
.It Fl t Ar TAG
Use the specified tag.
The default is the user name.
.It Fl p Ar PRIO
Set the priority as a number or a
.Ar FACILITY.LEVEL
pair.
.El
.Ss logread
.Bl -tag -width "-F"
.It Fl f
Output data as the log grows.
.It Fl F
Dump the buffer, then output data as the log grows.
.El
.Ss syslogd
.Bl -tag -width "-R HOST[:PORT]"
.It Fl n
Run in the foreground.
.It Fl R Ar HOST Ns Op : Ns Ar PORT
Log to the specified host and port.
The default port is 514.
.It Fl L
Log locally as well as over the network.
With
.Fl R ,
the default is to log only over the network.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /dev/bootlog
.It Pa /dev/bootlog
Rach2 kernel log.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Dl logger "hello"
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr bootlog 4
.Sh HISTORY
